      <p class="label"><a href="guides.html">Guides</a> · For artists</p>
      <h1>How to get your synthwave track on <em>radio and playlists</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">Synthwave has a big network of independent radio shows, playlists and blogs. Here's how to reach them, and how to avoid paying for nothing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="side">
    <div class="wrap guide-body">

      <h2>1. Get ready before you pitch</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>A finished, mastered track.</b> Curators judge in the first 30 seconds, so put your strongest moment early.</li>
        <li><b>A link that plays straight away:</b> Spotify, Bandcamp, SoundCloud or YouTube. Avoid downloads that need a sign-in.</li>
        <li><b>Your artwork and a short bio</b> (two or three lines: who you are, your sound, where you're from).</li>
        <li><b>Your social handles,</b> so curators can tag you when they play you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Pick the right curators</h2>
      <p>Ten curators who love your style beat a hundred who don't. Before you send anything:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Listen to a few recent episodes or the latest playlist adds. Do they play tracks like yours?</li>
        <li>Check the genre: synthwave, darksynth, outrun, dreamwave and synthpop audiences are not the same.</li>
        <li>Read their submission rules and follow them exactly. Many shows ignore pitches that break them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Write a short, personal pitch</h2>
      <p>Keep it to a few lines. Say who you are, what the track is, why it fits <i>their</i> show or playlist, and include the link. Mention an episode or track of theirs you liked. Don't send attachments unless they ask.</p>

      <h2>4. Pay for a listen, never for placement</h2>
      <p>Paid submission services are fine when you're paying for a <b>guaranteed listen and honest feedback</b>. Be careful with anyone who <b>guarantees streams or playlist adds for money</b>: Spotify forbids paying for playlist placement, and fake streams can get your music removed.</p>

      <h2>5. Follow up and say thank you</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>If you're played, share it and tag the show. Curators remember artists who promote their shows.</li>
        <li>If you're declined, read the feedback. It's often the most useful thing you'll get all year.</li>
        <li>Keep a simple list of who played you, so you can send them your next release first.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="panel box guide-cta">
        <p class="label">The easy way</p>
        <h3>RRR Submit: radio, playlists and blogs in one place</h3>
        <p>Send your track to the RRR radio show, the #synthfam playlist and other synth curators for <b>€1.50 per curator</b>. Every curator plays it and gives you a written answer within 14 days, or you get that part of your money back. You pay for a listen and honest feedback, never for placement.</p>
